.TH FOPEN 3S "August 1, 1992"
.ds ]W ANSI C
.SH NAME
fopen, freopen, fdopen \- open a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.SM
\fBFILE *fopen(const char *\fIfilename\fP, const char *\fItype\fP);
.PP
.SM
.B FILE
\fB*freopen(const char *\fIfilename\fP, const char *\fItype\fP, FILE *\fIstream\fP);
.PP
.SM
\fBFILE *fdopen(int fildes, char *\fItype\fP);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fopen
opens the file named by
.I filename
and associates a stream with it.
.I Fopen
returns a pointer to be used to identify the stream in subsequent operations.
.PP
.I Type
is a character string having one of the following values:
.TP 5
\*(lqr\*(rq
open for reading
.ns
.TP 5
\*(lqw\*(rq
truncate to zero length or create for writing
.ns
.TP 5
\*(lqa\*(rq
append: open for writing at end of file, or create for writing
.PP
In addition, each
.I type
may be followed by a \*(lq+\*(rq to have the file opened for reading and writing.
\*(lqr+\*(rq positions the stream at the beginning of the file, \*(lqw+\*(rq creates
or truncates it, and \*(lqa+\*(rq positions it at the end.  Both reads and writes
may be used on read/write streams, with the limitation that an
.IR fseek ,
.IR rewind ,
or reading an end-of-file must be used between a read and a write or vice-versa.
.PP
.I Type
may also be followed by a \*(lqb\*(rq to denote a binary file, though this
has no effect on this system.
.PP
.I Freopen
substitutes the named file in place of the open
.IR stream .
It returns the original value of
.IR stream .
The original stream is closed.  The 
.I type
argument is used just as in the 
.I fopen
function.
.PP
.I Freopen
is typically used to attach the preopened constant names,
.B stdin, stdout, stderr,
to specified files.
.PP
.I Fdopen
associates a stream with a file descriptor obtained from
.IR open ,
.IR dup ,
.IR creat ,
or
.IR pipe (2).
The
.I type
of the stream must agree with the mode of the open file.
The meaning of the 
.I type
flag is exactly as specified for 
.IR fopen ,
except that \*(lqw\*(rq and \*(lqw+\*(rq do not cause truncation
of the file.  The file position indicator associated with the
new stream is set to the position indicated by the file 
offset associated with the file descriptor.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
If successful,
.IR fopen ,
.IR freopen ,
and
.I fdopen
returns a pointer to a stream.  Otherwise
a 
.SM
.B NULL
pointer is returned
if
.I filename
cannot be accessed,
if too many files are already open,
or if other resources needed cannot be allocated.
.SH NOTES
.I Fdopen
is not portable to systems other than UNIX
(and is neither ANSI- nor POSIX-compliant).
.PP
The read/write 
.I types
do not exist on all systems.  Those systems without
read/write modes will probably treat the 
.I type
as if the \*(lq+\*(rq was not present.  These are unreliable in any event.
.PP
The current directory is specified when the string "" is given as 
\fIfilename\fR.
.SH BUGS
In order to support the same number of open files as does the system,
.I fopen
must allocate additional memory for data structures using
.I calloc
after 20 files have been opened.
This confuses some programs which use their own memory allocators.
An undocumented routine,
.IR f_prealloc ,
may be called to force immediate allocation of all internal memory
except for buffers.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
open(2),
fclose(3S)
